  "result": "I wrote the revised record for `NRS_484B_223_4a` to `record.jsonl`; it has not been through the gate again yet.\n\n**What the gate refused:** in the old rule, the right turn's target region `r` (an intersection or driveway entrance) was linked to the vehicle only through the declared intention. The gate does not count that as a link, so a planned right turn at any intersection or driveway in the scene would have satisfied the rule.\n\n**The fix:** the intention now points at the right turn lane `t` itself. That lane is already tied to the vehicle because the vehicle occupies it.\n- **New intention id:** `int.right_turn_from_lane`. It means the source's current plan has the vehicle leave that lane by a right turn at the lane's end.\n- **Why this reading:** the law's purpose (\"only for the purpose of making a right turn\") is the purpose of being in that lane, so the lane is the region the words name.\n- **Both cases still covered:** the turn can be into an intersection, a driveway or an alley entrance. I didn't use the coder's suggestion of `st.lane_enters`, because it only relates a lane to an intersection. A right turn lane that ends at a driveway would then have been judged violated, or exempt.\n- **Atoms dropped:** `st.intersection` and `st.driveway_or_alley_entrance` are gone from s3, since the rule no longer reads them.\n\n**Other changes:**\n- **\"Single\" (s1):** I recorded the word \"single\" as a residue that adds no restriction. Every turn lane is used only for turning, so the duty is the same whether or not a second right turn lane runs beside it. The validator might object to a residue on a span that also carries atoms.\n- **Scenarios:** the four required scenarios are kept, rewritten for the new intention. I added `branch_1`, a left turn lane (not applicable), and `branch_2`, a right turn lane ending at a driveway with the turn declared (compliant).\n\nThe coder will need to update the rule and witnesses, which still use `int.right_turn` with an intersection or driveway as the target.",
